    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d205-a3">

                <pb facs="0480" n="384" sameAs="frus:frus1926v02/pg384"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure 3—Translation<anchor n="22"
                            xml:id="d205-f1"/>]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Greek Minister for Foreign Affairs</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Roufos</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the American
                        Minister</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Laughlin</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Athens</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-02-17"><hi rendition="#italic">February 17,
                        1926</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 4976</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Minister</hi>: In reply to notes Nos. 7<anchor
                        n="23" xml:id="d205-f2"/> and 11 which you were pleased to address to me on
                    January 25 and February 1 of this year and with reference to <hi
                        rendition="#italic">note verbale</hi> of this Ministry No. 2991, of January
                    30, concerning the recent loan of January 24, 1926, I have the honor to reply as
                    follows:</p>
                <p>The loan in question, not affecting either deposits or credits and intended
                    especially to reach institutions of deposit and not private deposits, cannot be
                    considered as a forced loan of the kind covered in the treaties now in force. In
                    reality, a forced loan is not involved in this case, but only a fiscal or
                    monetary measure, rigorously limited to the money now in circulation.
                    Consequently, in view of the nature and method of its operation, no exemption
                    can be made in favor of foreign nationals.</p>
                <pb facs="0481" n="385" xml:id="pg385"/>
                <p>As to the question of the exemption of consuls of career, this is covered
                        in<anchor n="24" xml:id="d205-f3"/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">note verbale</hi> No. 2991.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">L. Kanakaris
                    Roufos</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d205-f1" n="22" xml:id="d205-f1-content">
